#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084121} :activity/id #uuid "77e2555f-bdb8-5497-9a3c-5271eebf55c0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049434 :topic/id #uuid "f515510e-ad31-5972-8142-51eb925f2189" :topic/math-academy-id 705} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084449 :tutorial/content "A **combination** is a selection of objects in which order does *not* matter. Two selections of the same items are always counted as the same selections, even if the items are in a different order.\n\nFor example, suppose we have $5$ books but can only take $3$ of them on a trip. How many combinations are there? In other words, how many ways can we take $3$ of the books?\n\nFirst, recall that the total number of ways of selecting $3$ books from $5$ (where order matters) is given by the permutation ${}_5P_3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!} \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the permutation calculation ${}_5P_3$ assumes that order of selection matters, but in our problem order is not important. Regardless of the order in which we choose the books, the chosen books are coming with us on the trip and the rest of the books will be left behind.\n\nSo, we must divide by the number of ways to order the $3$ books that we selected, which is $3!.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{}_{5}{P}_{3}}{3!} & =\\frac{60}{3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{6} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $10$ ways that we can take $3$ of the books with us." :tutorial/id #uuid "4e5b5b6a-e449-5f00-9d3a-db539975a666" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3684 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d37a45bc-ec71-5ab7-9073-0fdd10da2df9" :step/math-academy-id 20574 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084450 :tutorial/content "In general, the number of unique combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\frac{{}_nP_r}{r!} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}.\n$$\n\nThis is pronounced \"en-see-arr.\" The \"C\" stands for \"combination.\" \n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books, taking $r=3$ books with us on the trip. Using the formula, the total number of combinations is computed as\n![](images/e7/e73f73afd887785d06875907181fa38097aac62a65a81f5da464b674763ff863.svg)\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** An alternative notation for ${}_nC_r$ is $\\displaystyle {{n}\\choose{r}}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0e346d4a-6e9b-5361-b1da-2f42cbe10deb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3685 :tutorial/title "The General Formula for Combinations"} :step/id #uuid "a20af0b3-5236-5d21-b614-d269c0d8d5f8" :step/math-academy-id 20575 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084133}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083224 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083024 :question/id #uuid "e03fa018-0d27-5038-8c77-cf6bb723f9cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392770}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"} :step/id #uuid "820ae5b8-8732-577b-8c88-d57ca0748449" :step/math-academy-id 20576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083025 :question/id #uuid "e87f2616-514d-5b27-9f41-055c1f32f0cf" :question/math-ac